  "case_id": "xrepo-v6-6-a-link-is-matched-not-parsed-medium",
  "case_question": "Several libraries in this folder have to answer a yes/no question about a piece of structured text before they can act on it: does this target point somewhere else entirely, or is it relative to where we already are; is this host one of the hosts we are willing to embed or answer for; which file does this recorded location refer to. In each case the answer is reached by pattern-matching the text itself - a substring, a suffix, a glob turned into an expression, an unanchored search - rather than by handing the string to the parser that owns its grammar and comparing the parsed parts. Every one of those matches is right for the examples its author had in mind and wrong for inputs that are perfectly legal: a target that merely carries another target inside its query string is treated as though it pointed away; a name that only ends with the trusted name, or that reaches it over the wrong scheme, is accepted as though it were the trusted one; a recorded location whose path contains a space falls out of the pattern entirely and is read as a different location. The consequences differ by library - a prefix silently dropped, a stranger trusted, a caller mislabelled - but the defect is one contract, and elsewhere in the folder the same decision is made the right way: one library refuses to reason about a target at all until the platform parser has normalised it, and another anchors its dangerous-prefix test to the start of the string and spells out, with the specification cited, exactly which characters the grammar lets an attacker hide in there. Which files decide what a piece of structured text refers to by matching it, and which own the parse-first contract they have to be brought in line with?",
